ServiceNow Flow


---

UI Side

User fills the onboarding form on the portal → Redux dispatches an action → root-saga.ts catches that action and decides which backend API to call based on the user submission.

These saga functions internally call APIs defined in the UI api-store.


File:
portal-ui/src/api-store/root-saga.ts
(Along with other saga files in pages/.../saga.ts)


---

Backend Side

1. API Router receives the request

Once the UI calls an endpoint (e.g., /serviceNow/add_user_to_pod), the main FastAPI router receives it first.


File:
portal-backend/src/web/api/router.py


---

2. Main router forwards to module-specific router

Based on the URL prefix (/serviceNow in this example), the main router hands the request to the appropriate feature router (ServiceNow router).


File:
portal-backend/src/web/api/servicenow/servicenow_user_routes.py
(or servicenow_pod_routes.py depending on feature)


---

3. Feature router calls the appropriate backend service

Inside servicenow_user_routes.py, each endpoint (e.g., @router.post("/add_user_to_pod")) calls a corresponding method in the ServiceNow service class.

This service contains the business logic for creating/updating SNOW tickets, retrieving sys_id, etc.


File:
portal-backend/src/services/servicenow/servicenow_user_service.py
(Or servicenow_pod_tasks.py depending on operation)


---

4. Service calls task/helper layer to talk to ServiceNow API

The service layer then uses ServiceNow task modules to send actual REST API calls to SNOW.

